# VARA — Licensing

VARA is a mixed software + open-hardware project and is **dual-licensed** by component, so each
part carries the license that fits it best.

| Component | Path(s) | License | File |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Software** | `app/`, `web/`, build/generator scripts (`*.py`, `*.js`) | **MIT** | [`LICENSE-MIT`](LICENSE-MIT) |
| **Documentation** | `docs/`, `*.md`, `README` | **MIT** | [`LICENSE-MIT`](LICENSE-MIT) |
| **Hardware design** | `cad/`, `electronics/` (CAD sources, STL/STEP/glTF, KiCad, netlist, BOM) | **CERN-OHL-S v2** | [`LICENSE-CERN-OHL-S-2.0.txt`](LICENSE-CERN-OHL-S-2.0.txt) |

## Why dual

- **MIT** keeps the software and documentation trivially reusable — anyone can read, learn from,
  copy, adapt, or build on the code with near-zero friction. Maximum reach for the parts where
  permissive reuse is the goal.
- **CERN-OHL-S v2** (Strongly Reciprocal) is the hardware copyleft: anyone may study, make, and
  modify the hardware design, but **derivatives of the hardware must be released under the same
  license, with their complete source** (CAD, schematics, BOM). This keeps improvements to the
  physical design open rather than absorbed into a closed product.

The tradeoff, stated plainly: permissive (MIT) maximises adoption but allows closed derivatives;
reciprocal (CERN-OHL-S) keeps derivatives open but is less convenient to combine with proprietary
work. The split applies each where it matters — open, reusable code; open, self-perpetuating
hardware.

## SPDX

```
SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT                  (software + docs)
SPDX-License-Identifier: CERN-OHL-S-2.0       (hardware design)
```

## Honesty note (not a legal term)

These licenses cover the **design files**. They make no claim that the design has been
manufactured or verified — it is **"validated-by-design," not hardware-tested** (see the README).
Build at your own risk and verify before fabricating.

